## EmitDiagnostics body and emit loop

Shipped in Slice 7b. Body short-circuits when `DiagnosticCounts::any_populated` returns false after flushing the sink; otherwise stages populated records into a stack-cap working array, sorts via the host-side approximation comparator with `original_slot_index` tiebreaker, walks the sorted array calling `EmitWriter::write_str` per formatted line, calls `flush` at end-of-emit. AccessSet extended per the Slice 7b DOC CL: `EmitDiagnostics<W: EmitWriter>` generic; Read = `Cons<Resource<DiagnosticCounts>, Cons<Column<WuDiagnostic>, Empty>>`; Write = `Cons<Resource<DiagnosticSink<W>>, Empty>`.

## EmitWriter trait surface for viola-core

Shipped in Slice 7b. Minimal three-method trait: `write_str(&mut self, s: &str)`, `write_bytes(&mut self, bytes: &[u8])`, `flush(&mut self)`. Return type settled as **unit-return** per the Slice 7a-deferred call: the WU body has no useful recovery action on per-write failure; viola-cli's concrete impl handles syscall context internally. Pre-1.0 trade is observability-vs-simplicity; the trait can flip to `notko::Outcome<()>` later when a real recovery action surfaces (LSP-buffered emit, network-shaped sink).

## DiagnosticSink generic carrier flip

Shipped in Slice 7b. `pub struct DiagnosticSink<W: EmitWriter> { writer: UnsafeCell<W> }` with private `writer` field. `unsafe impl<W: EmitWriter> Sync` four-invariant SAFETY contract: EmitDiagnostics is sole writer (declared in its Write set); the scheduler serialises Write access; no other WU declares this Resource; interior mutability through `&self` is single-threaded under per-WU dispatch. `pub const fn new(writer: W) -> Self` lets viola-cli register the Resource with its concrete impl at scheduler-builder time.

## Resource<DiagnosticCounts> for per-L populated counts

Shipped in Slice 7b. `pub struct DiagnosticCounts { counts: UnsafeCell<[arvo::USize; MAX_LINTS]> }` with **no shared counter**. Distinct from the host-shim Resources: written at WU dispatch time by the parallel RunLint cohort, with each L writing only `counts[L]` (distinct slot indexes; the disjoint-slot strategy is the only synchronisation). A sibling shared `populated` counter would have been a Cell read-modify-write race under the parallel cohort regardless of end-state commutativity. The `any_populated` 32-entry scan lives on the read side where phase-separation guarantees no concurrent writer. **RunLint's Write set extends** to include this Resource (load-bearing AccessSet change to the closed Slice 6b WU; landed inside Slice 7b's scope per the Slice 7a DOC CL framing). `pub unsafe fn set_count` host-shim seam for RunLint-side writers; safe `count_at` and `any_populated` readers for EmitDiagnostics.

**Slice 9 audit precondition**: parallel fan-out verification re-checks COMMUTATIVE commutativity against the extended Write set. Per-L writes to `counts[L]` for distinct L touch disjoint slot indexes by construction; no shared scalar is touched; commutativity holds; Slice 9 walks the cohort to confirm.

## Host-side WuDiagnostic sort approximation

Shipped in Slice 7b. Comparator sorts by `(source, severity, range.start_line, range.start_col, original_slot_index)` with the `original_slot_index` tiebreaker making the comparator total. Insertion sort over the stage prefix is the algorithm choice: stable, simple, no_std-friendly, dominated by per-record emit cost for typical N. Stability is theoretically redundant under a total comparator, but the stable + total pair is the safer combination than non-stable + total. The host-side approximation is a documented deviation from ABI §10's canonical sort; canonical restoration waits on the Slice 2b host-shim long-lived interner BACKLOG entry shipping the LintFinding carrier with interned path / plugin_id / rule_id fields.

## EmitDiagnostics surface choice hardcoded

Shipped in Slice 7b. Human-readable emit format hardcoded: one line per diagnostic in `"[severity] source: <no message>\n"` shape (range fields stay placeholders pending the LintFinding carrier upgrade; message is `Str::default()` placeholder for the same reason). JSON / LSP emit formats defer to a future slice that introduces `Resource<RunSurface>` (already on the Slice 6a BACKLOG) and branches the emit on the surface variant.

## viola-cli Scheduler::run Outcome handling

Shipped in viola-cli Scheduler::run Outcome handling slice: viola-cli's unix-main matches `Outcome::Ok(_) -> EXIT_OK`, `Outcome::Err(_) -> EXIT_PLUGIN`. Hilavitkutin's engine body (hilavitkutin PR #83 at hilavitkutin dev `c040b4d`) returns `Outcome::Ok(())` cleanly as the no-op transitional shape; the `Err` arm is unreachable in practice until the real morsel loop body lands (the LLVM-plugin-gated follow-up tracked in hilavitkutin's `Scheduler::run real morsel loop body` BACKLOG entry), but the arm stays in place for forward compatibility per pre-1.0 churn discipline.

## viola-cli scheduler wiring and concrete EmitWriter impl

Shipped in Slice 8b.2: the full scheduler-build chain landed in viola-cli's unix-main. The complete chain registers twelve Resources (DiscoveredFilePaths, DiagnosticCounts, DiagnosticSink<StderrEmitter>, Workspace, RunSurface, ConfigBytes, ViolaCfg, ExtensionHost, LintSlots, LintConfigBuffer, CiState, FileEntryBuffer), four Columns (FileInfo, WuDiagnostic, PluginEntry, Nam), five unit-WU instances (LoadConfig, LoadPlugins, DiscoverFiles, RunRunner, EmitDiagnostics::<StderrEmitter>::new()), and the 32-instance RunLint cohort via a local `register_lints!` declarative macro that unrolls `.with(RunLint::<0>)` through `.with(RunLint::<31>)`. The chain ends with `.build()` then `.run()`. Three Resources were missing from the original BACKLOG estimate (Slice 8a wrote "nine Resources" but the WU AccessSet union gives twelve): ConfigBytes, RunSurface, ViolaCfg. The slice also promotes `EmitWriter` to `EmitWriter: Send + Sync` so the `Resource<DiagnosticSink<W>>` Sync proof completes; raises viola-cli's `recursion_limit` to 1024 for the deep cons-list typestate proofs; and adds `impl Default for ConfigBytes` in viola-config so the buffer registers without inline bare-numeric literals. Runtime execution waits on `hilavitkutin::Scheduler::run`'s body landing (currently `unimplemented!()` per the Pass 6 signature commitment); the wiring proves the AccessSet contracts at compile time regardless of runtime executability.

## ExtensionHost re-export disambiguation

Shipped in Slice 8b.1: dropped re-export. `viola_core::lib.rs` no longer re-exports `ExtensionHost` from `hilavitkutin_extensions`. Consumers split: the substrate extension host (`hilavitkutin_extensions::ExtensionHost`) is for plugin-loading via `host.load(...)`; the Resource version (`viola_core::resources::ExtensionHost`) is for the scheduler-builder's `Resource::new(host)` call. Two distinct concepts, two distinct paths. The other sibling re-exports from `hilavitkutin_extensions` (`ProviderId`, `Extension`, `ExtensionMeta`, `ExtensionRequirement`, etc) stay because they are non-colliding plugin-loading surfaces.
